## Context

Phase 1.3 of the bounded-memory filesystem connector epic (gossip-8936). The previous phases establish:
- Phase 1.1: Sorted DFS walk (per-directory sort → globally sorted output)
- Phase 1.2: Spill-to-disk when the walk buffer exceeds memory limits (bounded buffer → k-way merge of sorted runs → single merged run file)

This task introduces a **`SortedIndex`** abstraction that lets the filesystem connector's serving layer (pagination, split-point selection, binary search, membership verification) operate identically over both in-memory and on-disk index representations. The in-memory variant preserves current behavior exactly. The on-disk variant uses the merged run file produced by Phase 1.2, accessed via `memmap2` for zero-copy reads.

Without this abstraction, the serving layer (`enumerate_page_bounds`, `choose_split_point_bounds`, `byte_weighted_split_idx`, `verify_membership`) directly indexes into `Vec<FileEntry>` and `Vec<u64>`, which requires the entire file index to fit in RAM — untenable at TB-PB scale (1B+ files × 140-260 bytes/entry = 140-260 GB).

