## Context

Phase 2.2 of the bounded-memory filesystem connector epic (gossip-8936). The current `FsScanDriver` and `FilesystemScanSourceFactory` create a bare `FilesystemConnector` with only the root path, ignoring the shard key-range bounds available on the `Assignment`. This means every worker walks and indexes the entire filesystem tree, regardless of which shard it owns — wasting O(N) memory per worker instead of O(N/shards).

The `Assignment` struct already carries `shard_spec: ShardSpec` with `key_range_start` / `key_range_end` bounds. The `FilesystemConnector`'s `enumerate_page` and `choose_split_point` methods already accept `ShardSpec` and correctly limit pagination to the shard range. However, the **index itself** contains all files — the shard bounds only filter at serving time, not at walk time. To achieve true O(N/shards) memory, the connector must know its shard bounds at construction time so the walk can skip entries (and eventually entire subtrees) outside the assigned range.

This task threads `ShardSpec` bounds from the `Assignment` through the `FilesystemScanSourceFactory` → `FsScanDriver` → `FilesystemConnector` chain. It adds a `with_shard_bounds(start, end)` builder method to `FilesystemConnector` that stores the bounds and uses them during indexing to filter the walk output to only entries within `[start, end)`. Phase 2.1 (subtree pruning during the walk itself) is a separate task that depends on this plumbing being in place.

