## Desired State

### 1. `FilesystemConnector::with_shard_bounds(start, end)` builder method

Add optional shard-bound fields to `FilesystemConnector` and a builder method:

```rust
pub struct FilesystemConnector {
    root: PathBuf,
    // ... existing fields ...
    /// Optional inclusive lower bound for index-time filtering.
    /// Empty slice means unbounded (include all keys from start).
    shard_start: Vec<u8>,
    /// Optional exclusive upper bound for index-time filtering.
    /// Empty slice means unbounded (include all keys through end).
    shard_end: Vec<u8>,
}

impl FilesystemConnector {
    /// Restrict the connector's index to entries within the key range `[start, end)`.
    ///
    /// When set, `ensure_indexed` filters the walk output to only entries whose
    /// encoded key is `>= start` and `< end`. Empty slices mean unbounded.
    /// This enables O(N/shards) memory per worker in distributed mode.
    ///
    /// Must be called before the first indexing-triggering call.
    #[must_use]
    pub fn with_shard_bounds(mut self, start: &[u8], end: &[u8]) -> Self {
        self.shard_start = start.to_vec();
        self.shard_end = end.to_vec();
        self
    }
}
```

### 2. Index-time filtering in `ensure_indexed`

After `walk_dir_collect_files` produces the sorted file list, apply the shard bounds to retain only entries within `[shard_start, shard_end)`:

```rust
// In ensure_indexed, after files are sorted:
if !self.shard_start.is_empty() || !self.shard_end.is_empty() {
    let start_idx = if self.shard_start.is_empty() {
        0
    } else {
        files.partition_point(|f| f.key.as_bytes() < &self.shard_start)
    };
    let end_idx = if self.shard_end.is_empty() {
        files.len()
    } else {
        files.partition_point(|f| f.key.as_bytes() < &self.shard_end)
    };
    // Retain only the shard's range.
    if start_idx > 0 || end_idx < files.len() {
        files.drain(..start_idx);
        files.truncate(end_idx - start_idx);
    }
}
```

This is an index-time filter (COLD path) — the filtering happens once during `ensure_indexed`, not on the HOT pagination path. The retained entries and prefix sums reflect only the shard's slice of the keyspace.

**Note:** This is a post-walk filter. Phase 2.1 (subtree pruning during the walk itself) will use these same bounds to skip entire directories whose key-prefix falls outside `[start, end)`, avoiding the I/O and memory cost of walking those subtrees. This task establishes the plumbing; Phase 2.1 optimizes the walk.

