### Building the directory prefix for `should_skip_subtree`

The directory prefix relative to root is needed in byte form. The walk already maintains `current_path` as an absolute `PathBuf`. The relative prefix is `current_path.strip_prefix(root)` encoded via the same byte encoding as keys (raw OsStr bytes joined by `/`). However, calling `encode_rel_path` for every directory is wasteful. Instead, maintain a running `prefix_bytes: Vec<u8>` alongside `current_path`:

```rust
// When pushing a directory component:
if !prefix_bytes.is_empty() { prefix_bytes.push(b'/'); }
prefix_bytes.extend_from_slice(entry.name.as_bytes());

// When popping:
// Truncate to the last `/` or clear if at root level
```

This avoids re-encoding from `PathBuf` on every directory entry.

### Correctness argument for `should_skip_subtree`

Given directory prefix `p` (relative to root), all keys under this directory are in the set `{ p/<child_name>[/<deeper>...] }`. The lexicographic range of all such keys is `[p/, p0)` where `0` = byte after `/` = `0x30`.

If `[p/, p0)` does not overlap with `[shard_start, shard_end)`, then no key under `p` can be in the shard, so the subtree is safe to skip.

Non-overlap of `[a, b)` and `[c, d)` means `b <= c` OR `d <= a`:
- `p0 <= shard_start` (entire subtree is below shard range), OR
- `shard_end <= p/` (entire subtree is above shard range)

When `shard_start` is `None` (unbounded): first condition can never be true.
When `shard_end` is `None` (unbounded): second condition can never be true.
When last byte of `p` is `0xFF`: cannot form `p0` — return `false` (conservative).

