### 1. `CompletedRanges` struct (new, in `gossip-connectors` or `gossip-contracts`)

A sorted, non-overlapping set of completed half-open key ranges `[start, end)`:

```rust
/// Sorted, non-overlapping set of completed key ranges.
///
/// Constructed from done-ledger completion records. Used by the filesystem
/// walker to skip directory subtrees that fall entirely within completed
/// ranges during incremental rescans.
///
/// # Invariants
/// - Ranges are sorted by start key (lexicographic byte order).
/// - No two ranges overlap or are adjacent (they are merged on insertion).
/// - Empty ranges (`start == end`) are never stored.
pub struct CompletedRanges {
    /// Sorted non-overlapping `[start, end)` intervals.
    ranges: Vec<(Vec<u8>, Vec<u8>)>,
}

impl CompletedRanges {
    /// Create an empty set.
    pub fn new() -> Self;

    /// Create from a pre-sorted, non-overlapping iterator of `[start, end)` ranges.
    /// Debug-asserts the sorted/non-overlapping invariant.
    pub fn from_sorted(iter: impl IntoIterator<Item = (Vec<u8>, Vec<u8>)>) -> Self;

    /// Insert a completed range, merging with adjacent/overlapping ranges.
    pub fn insert(&mut self, start: Vec<u8>, end: Vec<u8>);

    /// Returns `true` if the prefix + all possible suffixes fall within a completed range.
    ///
    /// A directory with key prefix `p` (e.g., `data/logs/`) maps to the
    /// half-open key range `[p, p_successor)` where `p_successor` is `p`
    /// with the last byte incremented (or the next longer key if the last
    /// byte is 0xFF). This method returns `true` only if a single completed
    /// range fully covers `[p, p_successor)`.
    ///
    /// # Conservative guarantee
    /// Returns `false` if there is ANY possibility of uncovered keys in the
    /// subtree. False negatives (returning false when the subtree is actually
    /// covered) are safe — they just cause unnecessary re-scanning. False
    /// positives would skip unscanned entries and are never produced.
    pub fn covers_subtree(&self, prefix: &[u8]) -> bool;

    /// Returns `true` if the set is empty (no completed ranges).
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool;

    /// Returns the number of completed ranges.
    pub fn len(&self) -> usize;
}
```

