## Context

The commit pipeline worker in `run_commit_stage` checks the cancellation token
at the top of its loop before calling `recv_timeout`, and again after committing
a work item. There is a small race window: if cancel fires between the top-of-loop
check and the `recv_timeout` return, a buffered item can be dequeued and committed
post-cancel. Adding a single cancel check after dequeue but before commit closes
this window at zero cost.

This is not a correctness bug — the module docs at lines 232-235 explicitly
acknowledge the non-atomicity on the sender side, and persistence-layer epoch
fencing provides the real durability guard. This is a minor tightening of the
cancellation boundary that reduces the "post-cancel committed items" window from
"one in-flight + one dequeued" to "at most one in-flight."

## Current State

The worker loop in `run_commit_stage` (`commit_pipeline.rs:434-503`) checks
cancel at two points but not between dequeue and commit:

```rust
// crates/gossip-scanner-runtime/src/commit_pipeline.rs:434-451
loop {
    if cancel.is_cancelled() {                              // CHECK 1
        tracing::debug!("commit-stage worker exiting: cancellation observed before receive");
        break;
    }

    let work = match submit_rx.recv_timeout(CANCEL_POLL_INTERVAL) {
        Ok(work) => work,                                   // ← no cancel check here
        Err(RecvTimeoutError::Timeout) => continue,
        Err(RecvTimeoutError::Disconnected) => {
            tracing::debug!("commit-stage worker exiting: submission channel closed");
            break;
        }
    };

    let (write_context, completed_unit, translation) = work.into_parts();
    let expected_kind = completed_unit.checkpoint_boundary_kind();
    let result = committer.commit_translation(write_context, &completed_unit, &translation);
    // ... outcome handling ...
    if cancel.is_cancelled() {                              // CHECK 2
        // ...
    }
}
```

## Desired State

Add a cancel check immediately after `recv_timeout` returns `Ok(work)` and
before calling `commit_translation`:

```rust
let work = match submit_rx.recv_timeout(CANCEL_POLL_INTERVAL) {
    Ok(work) => work,
    Err(RecvTimeoutError::Timeout) => continue,
    Err(RecvTimeoutError::Disconnected) => { ... break; }
};

// Tighten the cancellation boundary: reject work dequeued after cancel
// rather than committing it. The item stays in-queue and is abandoned.
if cancel.is_cancelled() {
    tracing::debug!(
        "commit-stage worker exiting: cancellation observed after dequeue, before commit"
    );
    break;
}

let (write_context, completed_unit, translation) = work.into_parts();
```

Update the module-level cancellation docs (lines 33-52) to reflect the tighter
guarantee: the worker now exits before committing any newly-dequeued item when
cancel is set, not just before dequeuing more items.

